Number of partitions should be multiple of number of cores

So that no core remain idle

Application means a piece of code or multiple notebooks with complex logic

When user submits the application, driver initiates the spark session 

DAG creates logical plan 

DAG divides the code in small parts in form of tasks and give it to task executor

Executor requests for resources from cluster manager

Cluster manager allocates resources to execute tasks

Driver establishes connection with worker and assign tasks

Worker executes the task and return the result

Driver returns the result

Spark is fast because:

It has distributed frame work allowing it do parallel processing 

And it has in memory processing 

Driver and each executor in the worker have its own JVM processes

When application is submitted to spark, Spark divide the code into Jobs. Jobs are further divided into stages based on the number of shuffling operations across the cluster like Joins. No of stages determined by number of shuffle operations 

Inside each job, multiple tasks will be created

And each task perform same logic

No of tasks = No of paritions

For each partition, there’s a task

Transformation means it transforms the input RDD and create new RDD

RDD is the native structure of spark, it internally handles code as RDD

RDD or dataframe is stored in memory of cluster in form of partition

Each executor can consists of multiple cores

On heap memory and off heap memory 

