    - Spark executors
        
        resources - memory (RAM), CPU Cores (Compute)
        
        our intention is to make sure job should get the right amount of resources
        
        10 Node clusters means 10 worker nodes
        
        each node has:
        
        16 CPU cores (1 core will be allotted to background processes)
        
        64 GB Ram 
        
        Executor is like container of resources, it will hold ram and CPU
        
        one node can have more than 1 executor
        
        in a single worker node, we can have multiple executors == multiple executors
        
        container = CPU Cores + Memory (RAM)
        
        how many executor can be created in 1 node?
        
        strategies to create executor inside node:
        
        1. Thin/tiny executor - more executors, with each holding minimum resources
            1. 16 executor, each holding 1 core and 4 GB RAM
            2. In a single core- we will not have multi thread
            
            Drawback:
            
            1. in this scenario we will be losing the benefit of multi threading
            2. A lot of copies of broadcast variable are required. each executor should receive its own copy 
        2. Fat executor - less executors, with each holding max resources
            1. one executor of 16 CPU cores and 64 GB RAM
            
            Drawback:
            
            If a executor that holds more than 5 CPU cores, then the hdfs throughput suffers
            
            1. It is observed that if executor holds more than five CP course than the HDFS throughout suffers
            2. if executor holds huge amount of memory, then the garbage collection takes a lot of time
                1. garbage collection means removing unused objects from memory
    - A Little More on Spark Executors
        
        balanced approach
        
        from 16 cores and 64 GB RAM, 1 core and 1 GB RAM is given for background activities
        
        in each node we are now left with 15 Core and 63 GB RAM
        
        we don't want our HDFS throughput to suffer (it suffers when we use more than 5 core per executor)
        
        each executor should have ≥5 CPU cores
        
        and for multi threading we need >1 CPU Core per executor
        
        15 Core and 63 GB RAM
        
        now we have 5 core per executor
        
        so we will have 3 executor
        
        so each executor with 5 CPU CORE and 21 GB RAM
        
        out of which 7% goes in overhead so 1.5 GB goes there == Off heap memory
        
        so 21-1.5 = ~19 GB
        
        so each exeutoe having 5 CPU CORE and 19 GB RAM
        
        and each node has 3 executoe
        
        so total executor = 3x 10 = 30
        
        from which 1 executor goes to YARN applicatoin
        
        so 29 executor
        
